                <pb facs="0327" n="262" sameAs="frus:frus1936v03/pg262"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">124.84/63: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Bingham</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-05-04T00:00:00+01:00">May 4,
                        1936—midnight</date>.<lb/>[Received May 4—5:55 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>245. For Murray from the Ambassador. Since your telephone call<anchor n="26"
                        xml:id="d285-f1"/> the Foreign Office received a telegram from the British
                    Minister at Addis Ababa the substance which follows:</p>
                <p>The British Minister is unable to spare any men or guns for the defence of the
                    American Legation which is 5 miles away and outside any defence scheme and the
                    British Legation has already had to send a detachment of their guard to protect
                    the Belgian Legation whose occupation by the rioters would endanger the British
                    Legation. The British Legation has barely enough to ensure the defence for 1200
                    refugees of whom very few can be used as volunteers. The American Legation was
                    visited by an armed convoy from the British Legation last night and offered
                    evacuation which was refused except for their women.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bingham</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
